is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: PC0’s NIC eth0 (first hop link to r1 on segment E) is exhibiting physical/driver transmit faults.

Mechanism: During sustained TCP transfers, PC0’s eth0 generates bursts of “carrier” transmit errors, causing frames to be lost on the first hop (PC0→r1). TCP interprets this as congestion/loss, backs off, and retransmits; the stream “freezes” for a moment and then recovers, repeating cyclically. Light pings usually slip through and look fine, masking the issue during quick tests.

Localization: 
- PC0 eth0 shows TX carrier errors: ip -s link on pc0 reports TX carrier 1263 (errors 0, drops 0). 
- The peer side r1 eth4 has 0 errors/drops, and all intermediate links (r1<->r11<->r12<->PC12) are clean (no drops in tc or interface stats). 
- Iperf between PC0 and PC12 shows heavy, bursty TCP retransmits (e.g., 997 rets in 8s, sudden bitrate dips at 4–6s), matching intermittent first-hop loss.
- Routing/adjacencies (OSPF/EIGRP) are stable with no flaps; large MTU pings (1472 with PMTUD) succeed, so this is not an MTU/fragmentation issue.

Root cause: Intermittent L1/L2 fault on PC0’s NIC or its attachment (virtual tap/cable) causing transmit carrier errors.

Corrective fix: Remediate PC0’s interface/link. In practice: reseat/replace the PC0 NIC or cable (segment E), or rebuild/rebind the PC0 vNIC (ip link set eth0 down/up or restart the PC0 node). Ensure duplex/speed settings match the peer (both fixed full or autoneg) and monitor ip -s link; the TX carrier counter should stop increasing. After repair, sustained iperf should show near-zero retransmits and stable throughput. === END ===